Where each description comes from
---------------------------------

| craze                   | opencode (packages/opencode/src/tool/)                    |
|-------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| descriptions/read.txt   | read.txt                                                  |
| descriptions/write.txt  | write.txt                                                 |
| descriptions/edit.txt   | edit.txt                                                  |
| descriptions/grep.txt   | grep.txt                                                  |
| descriptions/glob.txt   | glob.txt                                                  |
| descriptions/bash.txt   | shell/shell.txt with shell/prompt.ts's bash values        |

opencode renders its shell description at run time from shell/shell.txt and
shell/prompt.ts. descriptions/bash.txt is that rendering for a bash shell,
the only kind craze runs: shell.txt with prompt.ts's bash values put in for
${intro} (prompt.ts:258-259), ${workdirSection} (prompt.ts:260-261) and
${commandSection} (bashCommandSection, prompt.ts:78-119, whose ${chain} is
chainGuidance's bash sentence, prompt.ts:75). The values that depend on the
machine stay placeholders, which the bash tool renders with tool.Render:
${os}, ${shell}, ${tmp}, ${defaultTimeoutMs}, ${maxLines} and ${maxBytes}.
Rendering opencode's own ShellPrompt.render for a bash shell, with those six
left as placeholders, gives descriptions/bash.txt byte for byte except for
the edits below.

Every other file is the upstream file byte for byte except for the edits
below.


Edits to the descriptions
-------------------------

The edits allowed are exactly these (plan 019 section 3.2): remove the two
false claims that edit and write refuse a file the model has not read;
remove sentences naming tools craze does not have; replace "opencode" with
"craze"; render ${...} placeholders; add the sentences the behavioural
differences below require. Nothing else is reworded: the text is the tuned
asset.

| file      | original                                                                                                                  | now                                                                                                                                                           | why |
|-----------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| read.txt  | "- This tool can read image files and PDFs and return them as file attachments."                                          | "- This tool cannot read image files or PDFs yet."                                                                                                            | difference: images and PDFs are refused, not attached |
| write.txt | "This tool will fail if you did not read the file first." (end of the fifth line)                                         | removed; the instruction before it, to read the file first, stays                                                                                            | false: nothing enforces reading first (D-12, D-42) |
| edit.txt  | "This tool will error if you attempt an edit without reading the file." (end of the fourth line, with its trailing space) | removed; the instruction before it, to read the file first, stays                                                                                            | false: nothing enforces reading first (D-12, D-42) |
| grep.txt  | "- When you are doing an open-ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead" | removed                                                                                                                                                 | names the Task tool, which craze does not have |
| glob.txt  | "- When you are doing an open-ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead" | removed                                                                                                                                                 | names the Task tool, which craze does not have |
| bash.txt  | "If not specified, commands will time out after ${defaultTimeoutMs}ms." (ends the timeout bullet)                         | the same, followed by " The timeout cannot exceed 600000ms (10 minutes); a longer timeout is reduced to 600000ms."                                             | difference: a 10-minute cap |
| bash.txt  | (none: a new bullet after the timeout bullet)                                                                              | "  - When the command returns, every process it started that is still running is killed, so nothing it runs in the background (for example, with `&` or `nohup`) outlives the call." | difference: nothing outlives the call |
| bash.txt  | ${limits.maxLines}, ${limits.maxBytes} (JavaScript interpolations in prompt.ts)                                           | ${maxLines}, ${maxBytes}                                                                                                                                      | rendering: tool.Render's placeholder names are \w+ |

No description names opencode, LSP, WebFetch or TodoWrite, so the other
allowed edits change nothing. opencode fills ${tmp} with its own directory
(os.tmpdir()/opencode); craze's bash tool supplies craze's.


Parameter schemas
-----------------

Each tool's parameters are opencode's JSON Schema as its own snapshot test
pins it (packages/opencode/test/tool/__snapshots__/parameters.test.ts.snap),
written by hand in each Spec, less "$schema", which the wire does not carry.
Numbers must be JSON numbers: opencode removed string coercion
(read.ts:23-27), so "10" for a number is refused.


Deliberate differences in behaviour
-----------------------------------

Recorded in D-38 (discovery/native-harness/08-decisions.md) as well.

| opencode                                                        | craze                                                                                                                                               | why |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| bash's timeout has no maximum                                   | a 10-minute cap, stated in the description                                                                                                          | a hidden provider must not hold a turn for hours |
| bash's children may outlive the call                            | everything left in the command's session is killed when the command returns; the description says so                                              | no background jobs (Q5) |
| bash's exit code is in metadata only (shell.ts:585-594)         | also "exit code: N" in <shell_metadata> when it is not zero                                                                                         | a failed command with no output would otherwise read "(no output)" |
| read returns images and PDFs as attachments                     | refused: an image with "Cannot read image file yet: <path>"; a PDF with opencode's binary message, "Cannot read binary file: <path>", since opencode has no refusal of its own for one | images arrive with H8; the provider turns media results into a synthetic user message |
| write and edit write the file in place (edit.ts:155)            | a temp file renamed over it, under a per-path lock, with an external-change check just before the rename (inode, size and modification time, or still absent for a new file): "File changed on disk since it was read. Read it again before editing." | crash safety. The cost: a rewritten file gets a new inode, so hard links to it keep the old content, and its ownership, ACLs and extended attributes are not carried over; nothing is fsynced, so durability is not promised |
| edit's thresholds count UTF-16 units; replaceAll expands $& and $1 in newString (edit.ts:714) | thresholds count runes; the replacement is literal                                                                  | Go semantics; the second is a bug fix |
| a formatter, LSP diagnostics, file-watcher events               | not ported                                                                                                                                          | craze has no such subsystems |
| tree-sitter parsing of bash commands                            | not ported; the gate sees the raw command                                                                                                           | it exists only for permission patterns |
| apply_patch instead of edit and write for GPT-5 models          | not ported; every model gets edit and write                                                                                                         | a later profile, if wanted |
| a hidden "invalid" tool for unknown tool names                  | Fantasy's own error result: "tool not found: X. Available tools: ..."                                                                                | the same effect, with no extra tool |
| the doom-loop guard asks the user                               | a nudge, then the turn stops                                                                                                                        | there is no approval channel |
| a plugin hook sets bash's environment                           | craze's own provider credentials are removed from it                                                                                                | secrets |
| an external-directory permission                                | none                                                                                                                                                | no permission prompts (plan 019 decision 2) |
| system prompts per model family                                 | one system prompt                                                                                                                                   | D-30; a second profile carries the future |

Smaller differences found porting read and write:

| opencode                                                        | craze                                                                                                                                               | why |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| read cuts a line at 2000 UTF-16 units                           | at 2000 runes (Unicode code points), never inside a character                                                                                       | Go semantics, as for edit's thresholds |
| read sorts a directory's entries with localeCompare             | in byte order                                                                                                                                       | deterministic without a collation table |
| read's "Did you mean" takes names in readdir order              | in name order                                                                                                                                       | os.ReadDir sorts; deterministic |
| TextDecoder shows one U+FFFD per invalid UTF-8 sequence         | one per invalid byte                                                                                                                                | Go's decoder; differs only inside a truncated multi-byte sequence |
| read opens a FIFO or a device and waits or reads forever        | refused: "Path is not a regular file or a directory: <path>"                                                                                        | such a read ignores cancel and would hang the turn (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| read appends AGENTS.md instructions (<system-reminder>) and warms the LSP | neither                                                                                                                                   | instruction files arrive with H4-H6; no LSP |
| read and write may touch any file                               | <Home>/providers.toml is refused, by resolved path or as the same file: "Cannot access craze's credentials file"                                    | craze's own keys (plan 019 section 3.8) |
| write writes any content                                        | content holding the redaction marker is refused: "This text contains a redaction marker, not the file's real content. Re-read the file around the change and leave the credential untouched." | writing it back would put the marker on disk in place of the real key |
| a new file is 0666 and new parents 0777, less the umask         | a new file is 0644 and new parents 0755 (less the umask); an existing file keeps its mode, setuid, setgid and sticky bits included              | the rename sets the mode itself; the same result under the usual umask 022 |
| write through a symlink writes its target                       | the same, and the link is kept; a dangling link creates its target                                                                                  | a rename would otherwise replace the link |
| write fails on a file it cannot write                           | the same, checked before anything is written                                                                                                        | a rename needs only the directory, and would replace a read-only file |
| write to a directory or a special file fails from the OS        | "Path is a directory, not a file: <path>" (edit.ts:125's words) or "Path is not a regular file: <path>"                                             | a FIFO must never be opened to wait for a reader |
| write's result lists LSP errors after "Wrote file successfully." | "Wrote file successfully." alone                                                                                                                   | no LSP |
